        <h2 id="need-to-rekey" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Do You Actually Need to Rekey a Brand-New Home?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Yes. A brand-new house can have more outstanding keys by closing day than a decades-old resale home. Between the builder's site supervisor, framing and trim crews, the listing agent's lockbox, and anyone who walked through during showings, a "new" front door lock has already been touched by more hands than most homeowners realize.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Here's the part most new-homeowner advice skips. Production builders don't install high-security hardware for security's sake. They standardize on fast-rekey hardware, often Kwikset SmartKey-style cylinders, because it's faster and simpler for the builder's own locksmith to manage across an entire subdivision during construction. That convenience for the builder is exactly why a rekey should be your job on day one, not an optional upgrade.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you want the full breakdown of when rekeying is enough and when full replacement makes more sense, see our guide on <a href="/blog/moving-into-new-home-rekey-or-replace-locks/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">whether to rekey or replace your locks when moving into a new home</a>. This post focuses specifically on what's different about Smyrna's current new-construction wave.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-the-numbers" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Smyrna's New-Construction Boom, By the Numbers</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Smyrna's growth isn't new, but it hasn't slowed down either. The town's population more than doubled between 2000 and 2020, climbing from 5,679 to 12,883 residents, largely driven by the completion of Delaware Route 1 and the resulting development pressure along the Route 13 corridor.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
          <div class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
            <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-[#A61E22]">49</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1">active new-construction communities in Smyrna</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
            <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-[#A61E22]">17</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1">builders currently building in the Smyrna area</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 text-center">
            <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-[#A61E22]">2x</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1">population growth in Smyrna, 2000 to 2020</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          As of 2026, there are 49 active new-construction communities in the Smyrna area from 17 different builders, according to new-home listing data from <a href="https://www.newhomesource.com/communities/de/dover-area/smyrna" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NewHomeSource</a> and <a href="https://www.homes.com/new-homes/for-sale/smyrna-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Homes.com</a>. That volume of simultaneous construction means a lot of keys changing hands across town at any given time, which is exactly the environment where a move-in rekey matters most.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          In our own service calls across Smyrna's newer subdivisions, we see the same builder-grade SmartKey cylinders installed street after street within a single community. That's normal and not a defect. It just means every home on that street is running compatible, easily rekeyed hardware, which is worth knowing before you assume "new" equals "unique to you."
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We cover Smyrna as part of our regular <a href="/service-areas/smyrna-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Smyrna, DE service area</a>, including the newer subdivisions off Route 13 and the surrounding northern Kent County communities.
        </p>

        <h2 id="checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Your Smyrna Move-In Locksmith Checklist</h2>
        <img
          src="/images/move-in-locksmith-smyrna-de.jpg"
          alt="1st Capital Locksmith technician rekeying a front door deadbolt at a new construction home in Smyrna, DE"
          class="float-right w-1/2 max-w-[280px] h-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 ml-6 mb-4"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Whether you closed yesterday or you're closing next month, here's what to check before you consider move-in complete:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Rekey every exterior door.</strong> Front, back, side entry, the garage-to-house door. Don't stop at the front door.</li>
          <li>Reprogram the garage door opener too. Builders and past visitors may still have a working remote or code paired to it.</li>
          <li>What about the mailbox? Cluster mailboxes in newer Smyrna subdivisions often share a key format until you request a rekey.</li>
          <li><strong>HOA-managed access points</strong> are easy to forget. Many Smyrna communities run gated amenities, pool areas, or shared entries on their own key or fob system.</li>
          <li>Pick one spare key holder, not a hiding spot. A trusted neighbor or family member beats a spare hidden outside.</li>
          <li>Ask to key everything to one house key. One visit, one working key for every exterior lock.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="clear-both"></div>

        <h2 id="what-to-expect" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What Should You Expect From a Move-In Locksmith Visit?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A straightforward move-in visit covers how many doors you have and what hardware is already installed, and any locksmith worth hiring should walk you through that scope and confirm it with you before a technician ever knocks on your door. The real question to ask isn't how the visit is billed, it's whether the company will confirm the full scope of work with you in advance and stick to it once they arrive.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If a company won't confirm that scope over the phone, or the job changes dramatically once a technician arrives, that's a signal to call someone else, not a signal to go along with it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="avoid-scams" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">How Do You Avoid a Locksmith Scam When You Search "Locksmith Near Me" in Smyrna?</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The Better Business Bureau has documented a nationwide pattern of locksmith scams involving more than a thousand consumer complaints, where a business advertises a low online quote, then charges a much higher price once a technician is already at the customer's door (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Locksmith_scam" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia, Locksmith scam, 2026</a>). A related consumer advisory from <a href="https://www.yahoo.com/news/beware-locksmith-scams-lockout-215139808.html" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yahoo News</a> warns of the exact same bait-and-switch pattern during lockouts, when homeowners are most likely to accept a bad quote out of urgency.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Watch for these warning signs before you call anyone found through a quick search:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>No physical address in Delaware, or a generic national-sounding business name</li>
          <li>No local reviews, or reviews that only mention a call center, not a technician</li>
          <li>Refusal to give a firm quote over the phone before dispatching someone</li>
          <li>A dispatcher who answers with a generic greeting instead of a specific business name</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          For the full breakdown of scam red flags across Delaware, see our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-locksmith-scams/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">how to avoid locksmith scams in Delaware</a>.
        </p>

